<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 9</span>: </span><span id="A1.T9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Sensitivity to modulation rate <math id="A1.T9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="A1.T9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> In NoLan-Plus, <math id="A1.T9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is manually set to regulate the influence of the modulation distribution, defined in Equation <a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 3.2 No-Language-Hallucination Decoding ‣ 3 Method ‣ NoLan: Mitigating Object Hallucinations in Large Vision-Language Models via Dynamic Suppression of Language Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. When <math id="A1.T9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math>, NoLan-Plus reverts to standard decoding.</span></span></figcaption>
